    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we start by recalling a fundamental property of triangles: the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees.\n\nLet's denote the measures of the three interior angles of the triangle as \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\). According to the property mentioned, we have:\n\\[ A + B + C = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nThe mean value of the measures of the three interior angles is calculated by dividing the sum of the angles by the number of angles, which is 3. Therefore, the mean value is:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3} \\]\n\nSubstituting the sum of the angles into the equation, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} \\]\n\nPerforming the division, we find:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = 60^\\circ \\]\n\nThus, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
